Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 11   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Poles, Polars, and Projectivities for Conics",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["8 November 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Poles and Polars for Conics", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polarity with respect to a conic", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Definitions and basic properties", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Differentiation of a conic with respect to a point is an \
interesting and important transformation from points to lines.  The operation \
has a special name.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a conic, then ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,p]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a line, called the ",
  StyleBox["polar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with respect to the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is called the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of this line.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Two points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to a conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " if each is on the polar of the other.  Algebraically this means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ q] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " All such definitions can be stated in a ",
  StyleBox["dual form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Do this!)"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
The points on the conic have a special property.  The proofs are \
very easy.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " lies on its ",
  StyleBox["own polar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if, it lies on the conic; and in that case the polar ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the tangent to the conic at the point."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The polar of ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,p]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  If the point lies on the polar, then we have\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(0 == PD[PD[f, p], p] == PD[f, p\^2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nAnd so the point lies on the conic.  This proof is reversible.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If a point ",
  StyleBox["p ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the conic, then its polar is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tangent to the conic."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " The polar of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,p]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Suppose the polar ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a tangent to the conic.  Then there is a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the conic so that the polar represents the same line as the tangent at \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "That means we have an equation:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p] == a\ PD[f, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nDifferentiating both sides by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we find that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ q] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["on the curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Now differentiate both sides of the same equation by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and we find that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["f",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "And so the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the conic.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
From the above and from previous discussions of tangents we can \
conclude:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The polar of a point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " intersects a non-degererate conic at the ",
  StyleBox["two points of tangency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the tangents from ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to the conic.  The points of intersection are ",
  StyleBox["equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if, the point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the conic (and, therefore, on the polar)."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
Note we can turn this conclusion around:  if we are given are two \
distinct points on a non-degenerate conic, then the line joining the two \
points is the polar of the intersection of the two tangents at the points.    \
But there is a more general result, since the points do not have to lie on \
the conic.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a non-degenerate conic and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are two distinct points, then ",
  StyleBox["PE[q,r]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must represent the same line as ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,PE[PD[f,q],PD[f,r]]]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  In words we can say that:\n ",
  StyleBox[
  "The intersection of the polars of two points is the pole of the line \
joining them.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Because all non-degenerate conics are ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we can be content with a symbolic proof for a ",
  StyleBox["particular conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, q, r, Subscript]; \nf = x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2; \n
    q = a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + c\_1\ w; \nr = a\_2\ u + b\_2\ v + c\_2\ w; \n
    Tidy[PE[q, r]]\), 
    \(Tidy[1\/8\ PD[f, PE[PD[f, q], PD[f, r]]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The lines represented are seen to be the same.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But there is an even ",
  StyleBox["more general",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " statement continuing what was just proved."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Corollary.",
  StyleBox["  If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\)]],
  StyleBox[" is a non-degenerate conic and the point ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(q\)]],
  StyleBox[" moves along a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["line",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[", then the polars ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, q]\)]],
  StyleBox[" are all ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  " at the pole of the line.  This transformation from a range of points to a \
pencil of lines",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["  preserves crossratios",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof:",
  StyleBox["  The transformation  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q \[Rule] PD[f, q]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "linear transformation",
  StyleBox[" from the vector space ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[u, v, w]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" to ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[x, y, z]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  ".  Hence, collinear (dependent) points must go over into concurrent lines \
and crossratios must be preserved.  (Why?)  From the formula of the last \
theorem we identify this point of concurrency as the pole of the line \
containg the points ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As was pointed out earlier, there are going to be many consequences from \
this theorem involving ",
  StyleBox["crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Examples.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We illustrate the theorem with some pictures.  First a hyperbola."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = 2\ x\ y + 3\ y\ z + x\ z; \)\), 
    \(q[t_] := t\ \((\(-2\)\ u + w)\) + \((1 - t)\)\ \((\(-v\) + w)\); \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, PE[q[0], q[1]] == 0}, 
          Table[PD[f, q[t]] == 0, {t, \(-3\), 3}]] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        Join[{{Thickness[0.005], Red}, {Thickness[0.005], Blue}}, 
          Table[Black, {7}]]]; \)}], "Input"],

Cell["And we can change the conic to an ellipse.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^2\/4 + y\^2\/2 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(q[t_] := t\ \((\(-2\)\ u + w)\) + \((1 - t)\)\ \((\(-v\) + w)\); 
    \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, PE[q[0], q[1]] == 0}, 
          Table[PD[f, q[t]] == 0, {t, \(-3\), 3}]] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        Join[{{Thickness[0.005], Red}, {Thickness[0.005], Blue}}, 
          Table[Black, {7}]]]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Or we can let the point move along a diagonal line outside the \
conic. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^2\/3 + y\^2\/5 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(q[t_] := w + 4\ u + t\ \((w + 4\ v)\); 
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, PE[q[0], q[1]] == 0}, 
          Table[PD[f, q[t]] == 0, {t, \(-5\), 5}]] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-5\), 10}, {y, \(-5\), 7}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        Join[{{Thickness[0.005], Red}, {Thickness[0.005], Blue}}, 
          Table[Black, {11}]]]; \)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Self-polar triangles\t", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
Polarity with respect to a conic can create many special \
configurations of points and lines.  Here is one typical one.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A triangle of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is said to be ",
  StyleBox["self-polar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to a conic",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " if each side is the polar of the opposite vertex (which is the same as \
saying that any two distinct vertices are conjugate).  Algebraically this \
means that  \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\ q] == PD[f, q\ r] == PD[f, p\ r] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Self-polar triangles arise in configurations involving quadrangles, \
for example.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The three ",
  StyleBox["diagonal points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a non-degenerate ",
  StyleBox["quadrangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with all four of its vertices on a conic always form a ",
  StyleBox["self-polar triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We can do the calculation with respect to ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " convenient conic.  So here are four ",
  StyleBox["symbolic points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (which we can assume are distinct) on the ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p, t, d, Subscript]\), 
    \(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \n
    p\_1 = \((t\_1\%2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\_1\ v + \((t\_1\%2 + 1)\)\ w; 
    p\_2 = \((t\_2\%2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\_2\ v + \((t\_2\%2 + 1)\)\ w; 
    p\_3 = \((t\_3\%2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\_3\ v + \((t\_3\%2 + 1)\)\ w; 
    p\_4 = \((t\_4\%2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\_4\ v + \((t\_4\%2 + 1)\)\ w; 
    Expand[\((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_1, p\_2, p\_3, p\_4}]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
We compute the three diagonal points (no need to see the symbolic \
answers).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(d\_1 = PE[PE[p\_1, p\_3], PE[p\_2, p\_4]]; \n
    d\_2 = PE[PE[p\_1, p\_4], PE[p\_2, p\_3]]; \n
    d\_3 = PE[PE[p\_1, p\_2], PE[p\_3, p\_4]]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["We now check that we have a self-polar triangle.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[{PD[f, d\_1\ d\_2], PD[f, d\_2\ d\_3], PD[f, d\_3\ d\_1]}]\)], 
  "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is true!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Note that the parameterization leaves out ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on the conic.  (Why?)  But this does not reduce generality since we \
are only concerned with a finite number of points on the curve."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
And here is a picture with special values for the parameters. \
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t\_1 = 0; t\_2 = 1; t\_3 = 2; t\_4 = \(-20\); \n
    ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, \n\t\tPD[f, d\_1] == 0, PD[f, d\_2] == 0, 
          PD[f, d\_3] == 0, \n\t\t\ \ PE[p\_1, p\_3] == 0, 
          PE[p\_2, p\_4] == 0, \n\t\ \ \tPE[p\_1, p\_4] == 0, 
          PE[p\_2, p\_3] == 0, PE[p\_1, p\_2] == 0, PE[p\_3, p\_4] == 0} /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-2\), 5}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, \n\t
      Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.004], Red}, Green, Green, Green, Black, Black, 
          Black, Black, Black, Black}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " This picture shows us one way of constructing polars: Take a point.  By \
drawing two convenient lines, make it a diagonal point of a quadrangle with \
vertices on the conic.  Now ",
  StyleBox["join",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the the other two diagonal points.  Remember, the polar intersects the \
conic at the points the tangents from the pole touch the conic.  So, the \
picture also shows how to find these points from a quadrangle that is easy to \
draw."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["Let us consider some special cases.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The first case of a ",
  StyleBox["degenerate conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a pair of ",
  StyleBox["distinct lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".",
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "As a typical case we can take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["f",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(x\ y\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " which gives us the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "- and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axes.  We find a degeneracy because the polars are always lines passing \
through ",
  StyleBox["the origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(or, more generally, theought the intersection of the two lines)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[x\ y, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[b\ x + a\ y, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[x\ y, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
In this case, if four points lie two-by-two on the two lines (none \
at the intersection), one of the diagonal points is the intersection, and the \
two sides of the self polar triange passing through the intersection divide \
the two given lines harmonically.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[w, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RL[x, y, b\ x + a\ y, \(-b\)\ x + a\ y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The typical case of a line ",
  StyleBox["counted twice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(y\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which give the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis counted twice.  There is only one polar."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[y\^2, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "All points on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis are double points.  This is too degenerate to make interesting \
configurations."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We calculate next the polar of the non-degenerate ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to an arbitrary point (and divide out an unwanted numerical \
factor):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(p = a\ u + b\ v + c\ w; \)\), 
    \(Tidy[1\/2\ PD[f, p]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is quite a nice formula.  As we have proved, this line passes through \
the points where the tangents from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " meet the conic.  To find algebraically the ",
  StyleBox["points of tangency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] and the tangents themselves \[LongDash] we should be able to \
solve for the intersection of this line with the conic.  We have programmed a \
general routine for doing so, called ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["ConicTangents",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?ConicTangents\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(qq = ConicTangents[f, a\ x + b\ y - c\ z]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The general formula for the case of the circle is not so bad (but \
the program will work with any conic).  Let us check that the points are on \
the circle.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Together[Expand[PD[f, qq\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2]]]
      \), 
    \(Together[Expand[PD[f, qq\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2]]]
      \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here are the formulae for the ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(tan\_1 = 
      Tidy[Together[
          Expand[1\/2\ PD[f, qq\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]\ 
              \((a\^2 + b\^2)\)]]]\), 
    \(tan\_2 = 
      Tidy[Together[
          Expand[1\/2\ PD[f, qq\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]]\ 
              \((a\^2 + b\^2)\)]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, let us look at the ",
  StyleBox["intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two tangents."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PE[tan\_1, tan\_2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we get back to the ",
  StyleBox["original point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We draw pictures for several ",
  StyleBox["specific points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(polars = 
      \((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{\(-2\)\ u - 2\ v + w, 2\ u + 5\ v + w, 
          \(-4\)\ u + 3\ v + w}; \)\), 
    \(\(tans = \((ConicTangents[f, #1]&)\)/@polars; \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Flatten[{f == 0, \((#1 == 0&)\)/@polars, 
            \(({PD[f, #1\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]] == 0, 
                    PD[f, #1\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]] == 
                      0}&)\)/@tans}] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, \(-5\), 5}, {y, 
        \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {Black, Red, Green, Blue, Red, Red, Green, Green, Blue, 
          Blue}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here is an easy proof using ",
  StyleBox["pencils",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["duality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (The theorem could also be stated in dual form.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  If ",
  StyleBox["two pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of opposite sides of ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-degenerate quadrangle are conjugate with respect to a ",
  StyleBox["dual conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then so is the third side."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let the pairs of opposite sides be:  \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({{l\_1, l\_2}, {m\_1, m\_2}, {n\_1, n\_2}}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". \nThe products of the pairs are all (degenerate) conics passing through \
the four points.  But the conics through the four points form a ",
  StyleBox["pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Hence,  the following sort of linear relation must hold:  \n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(a\ l\_1\ l\_2 + b\ m\_1\ m\_2 == n\_1\ n\_2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now if  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as well as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", are conjugate with respect to a (dual) conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l\_1\ l\_2, g] == 0 == PD[m\_1\ m\_2, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nBut, by bilinearity, it follows at once that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[n\_1\ n\_2, g] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", as desired.",
  StyleBox[" Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  So, what does polarity with respect to ordinary ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-conics have to do with polarity with respect to dual ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-conics?  We answer this in the next section."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Hessians, dual conics, and correlations", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Relating polarity to the Hessian", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
As we have seen the pole of a line with respect to a conic can be \
determined by intersecting two polars of two points on the line; but we can \
also use the dual conic as well. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g = Envel[f]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "be the dual conic of a  ",
  StyleBox["non-degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then for points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p], g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " represents the ",
  StyleBox["same point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " , and\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, PD[l, g]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " represents the ",
  StyleBox["same line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nIn other words:\n",
  StyleBox[
  "The pole with respect to the dual conic of the polar of a point with \
respect to a given conic is the original point.  Polarity with respect to a \
conic is a one-one correspondence, and the inverse transformation is polarity \
with respect to the dual conic.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We will check this out by symbolic computation.  First, here is the \
general conic and its dual."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    f = Table[a\_i, {i, 6}] . Bxyz[2]\), 
    \(g = Envel[f]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now, take a general point and work out the first combination.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(p = a\ u + b\ v + c\ w; \nFactor[PD[PD[f, p], g]]\), 
    \(Expand[PE[p, %]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This computation establishes the result, because the factor we see for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[PD[f, p], g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is just twice the Hessian determinant."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[f]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we proved already that this quantitity is only ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " for the degenerate conics.  If we modify ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  by this constant factor, then we get a perfect match."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p = a\ u + b\ v + c\ w; \n
    Factor[PD[PD[f, p], g/\((2  Det[MHP[f]])\)]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Now for the other combination.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(l = a\ x + b\ y + c\ z; \nFactor[PD[f, PD[l, g]]]\), 
    \(Expand[PE[l, %]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["And, after modification:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l = a\ x + b\ y + c\ z; \n
    Factor[PD[f, PD[l, g/\((2  Det[MHP[f]])\)]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is the same story.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Note that this theorem is one way of showing that the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " given by the formula ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " indeed the envelope of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the dual envelope of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " again."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The Hessian matrix also makes the ",
  StyleBox["computation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of duals and poles and polars easy.  Consider a (slightly different form) \
of a general conic and its Hessian matrix, and note the ",
  StyleBox["equality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the following two computations:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, hes, a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    f = {a\_1, 2\ a\_2, a\_3, 2\ a\_4, 2\ a\_5, a\_6} . Bxyz[2]; \n
    hes = MHP[f]; \nTidy[PD[f, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]]\), 
    \(Tidy[{x, y, z} . hes . {a, b, c}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["In otherwords, we have proved:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If ",
  StyleBox["hes",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is is the Hessian matrix of a conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then when ",
  StyleBox["{a,b,c}",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["point coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we find that ",
  StyleBox["hes.{a,b,c}",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gives the ",
  StyleBox["line coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of the ",
  StyleBox["polar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to the conic. Therefore, the ",
  StyleBox["inverse matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["hes",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will transform",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["line coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " into point coordinates of the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  As the Hessian is a ",
  StyleBox["symmetric matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we can write the polar of a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u\  + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as ",
  StyleBox["{a,b,c}.hes",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as well.  The pole of  a line  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x\  + b\ y + c\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(Inverse[hes] . {a, b, c}\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox[\({a, b, c}\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[".",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(Inverse[hes]\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]]],
  ".  One problem with vector notation, however, is that you sometimes forget \
what the vectors stand for!"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Recall that we proved that if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a non-degenerate conic and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are two distinct points, then the pole of the line ",
  StyleBox["PE[q,r]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must be the same point as that represented by ",
  StyleBox["PE[PD[f,q],PD[f,r]]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the intersection of two polars.  We can check the property now by using \
the Hessian.  We can use for this example any particular non-degenerate \
conic.  We then calculate the pole of a line joining two points in two \
different ways."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
  \(ClearAll[conic, hes, a, b, c, d, e, f, p, q, r, \n\tSubscript]; \n
    conic\  = \ x\ y\  + \ y\ z\  + z\ x; \nhes = MHP[conic]; \n
    invhes\  = \ Inverse[hes]; \nq = \ a\ u + b\ v + c\ w; \n
    r = \ d\ u + e\ v + f\ w; \nline = Tidy[PE[q, r]]\), 
  \(pole = Tidy[2 {u, v, w} . invhes . CL[line]]\), 
    RowBox[{"Tidy", "[", 
      StyleBox[\(PE[PD[conic, q], PD[conic, r]]\),
        FontFamily->"Courier",
        FontWeight->"Bold"], 
      StyleBox["]",
        FontFamily->"Courier",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "Input"],

Cell["The two points are indeed the same.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The advantage of the Hessian is that the pole comes directly from the \
coordinates of a line without having to find two points on that line.  \
Finding ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Inverse[hes]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is, however, about as much work as finding the envelope of a conic.  Of \
course, finding envelopes applies as well to curves of ",
  StyleBox["higher degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Examples of dual conics and Hessians", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let us consider the general conic and its Hessian:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, g, hes, a, Subscript]; \)\), 
    \(f = {a\_1, 2\ a\_2, a\_3, 2\ a\_4, 2\ a\_5, a\_6} . Bxyz[2]; \n
    hes = MHP[f]; \nhes // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have just been using the inverse of the matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["hes",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to caculate poles of lines with respect to a given conic.  We simplify \
the inverse here to avoid fractions.  It is the answer to an interesting \
question, as we shall show."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(DH[hes_] := 
      Module[{hh, seq}, 
        seq := Flatten[
            hes]; {{\(-\((hh[5]\ hh[7])\)\) + hh[4]\ hh[8], 
              hh[2]\ hh[7] - hh[1]\ hh[8], 
              \(-\((hh[2]\ hh[4])\)\) + hh[1]\ hh[5]}, {
              hh[5]\ hh[6] - hh[3]\ hh[8], 
              \(-\((hh[2]\ hh[6])\)\) + hh[0]\ hh[8], 
              hh[2]\ hh[3] - hh[0]\ hh[5]}, {
              \(-\((hh[4]\ hh[6])\)\) + hh[3]\ hh[7], 
              hh[1]\ hh[6] - hh[0]\ hh[7], 
              \(-\((hh[1]\ hh[3])\)\) + hh[0]\ hh[4]}} /. 
          Thread[Table[hh[i], {i, 0, 8}] \[Rule] seq]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We check that it is indeed the multiple of the inverse matrix. \
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\((hes . DH[hes])\)/Det[hes] // ExpandAll\) // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, let us form the ",
  StyleBox["dual polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using this new matrix:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["g = Tidy[{u,v,w}.DH[hes].{u,v,w}/4]", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now compute the envelope of the conic using the general \
method:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Envel[f]", "Input"],

Cell["They are the same!  We have proved once again:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Given the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the dual ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " just constructed ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the Hessian matrix in fact represents the ",
  StyleBox["envelope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of transformations \n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p \[Rule] PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l \[Rule] PD[l, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\ndetermined by a conic and its dual map points to lines and lines to \
points.  These are ",
  StyleBox["linear transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and they preserve ",
  StyleBox["crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Also if point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["lies on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then \[LongDash] by what we have calculated \[LongDash] point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["lies on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". (Why?) This kind of transformational switch between points and lines is \
called a ",
  StyleBox["polarity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  And",
  StyleBox[" any such",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polarity is determined by a pair of dual conics (or a symmetric matrix).  \
(Why?)"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Metacomment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Polarities give us another view of the ",
  StyleBox["Principle of Duality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", since if a configuration of points and lines has some nice property, \
then the use of a polarity turns it into a dual configuration with the dual \
property."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The ",
  StyleBox["double dual property",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can also be checked as follows (provided the matrix ",
  StyleBox["hes",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is non singular):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Factor[DH[DH[hes]]]\/Det[hes]] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For a ",
  StyleBox["degenerate conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that factors into two lines, the Hessian is a singular matrix:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["hes = MHP[(x+ y + z)(x-y)]", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The dual is the square of the point of intersection of the two \
lines:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["g = Factor[{u,v,w}.DH[hes].{u,v,w}]", "Input"],

Cell["PE[(x+ y + z),(x-y)]", "Input"],

Cell["The double dual is then zero:", "Text"],

Cell["DH[DH[hes]]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Remark.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The dual of the degenerate conic ",
  StyleBox["x y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (as given by the minors of the Hessian) is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(w\^2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is the pencil of lines passing through the double point of the \
conic (and counted twice), so it seems that the dual equation is satisfied \
not only by the tangents, but rather by all the lines which have degree of \
contact ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or more with the conic.  If you take a square like ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", all the minors and hence the dual are zero, which is reasonable, since \
every line has degree of contact at least ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with a double line.  This means, however, that the dual of the dual of ",
  StyleBox["x y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", for example, is zero."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In complex numbers, it is not possible to give an equation in ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["w ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "that would be satisfied by exactly two lines.  One could do that in the \
real field -- for example, an equation satisfied by the coefficients of ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and of no other line would be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((v\^2 + w\^2)\)\ \((u\^2 + w\^2)\) == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Consider a ",
  StyleBox["dual conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be two fixed points.  What is the ",
  StyleBox["locus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " such that the lines joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ?   "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " It is a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We might as well take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in a standard form:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, a, b, c, Subscript]; \)\), 
    \(\(g = u\^2 + v\^2 + w\^2; \)\), 
    \(p = a\_1\ u + a\_2\ v + a\_3\ w; \nq = b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w; \n
    r = c\_1\ u + c\_2\ v + c\_3\ w; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The pair of lines we are interested in can be wrapped up as a ",
  StyleBox["degenerate conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lines = Tidy[Expand[PE[p, r]\ PE[q, r]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The condition about conjugacy means exactly that the expression ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[lines, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["vanishes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This gives us a ",
  StyleBox["quadratic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the coordinates of the point ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which we can express as a polynomial as follows as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = 
      Tidy[PD[lines, g] /. {c\_1 \[Rule] x, c\_2 \[Rule] y, c\_3 \[Rule] z}]
        \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We now check that both of the given points lie on this conic:\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Expand[PD[f, p\^2]], Expand[PD[f, q\^2]]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It should also be checked that ",
  StyleBox["every point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the conic comes up this way."
}], "Text"],

Cell["We will now prove by the methods we have developed the: ", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "he ",
  StyleBox["eight tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the ",
  StyleBox["four intersections",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of two conics are all tangent to ",
  StyleBox["another conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Consider a convenient (and non-degenerate) conic passing through some \
given points:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ff = 
      Tidy[1\/6\ P2P[\(-u\) + w, v + w, 2\ u + w, u + v + w, \(-v\) + w]]\), 
    \(Det[MHP[ff]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["It passes through the following four points by construction:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(pp = {\(-u\) + w, v + w, 2\ u + w, u + v + w}; \)\), 
    \(\((PD[ff, #1\^2]&)\)/@pp\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here are the tangents at those points:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tt = Tidy/@\(\((PD[ff, #1]&)\)/@pp\); \)\), 
    \(TableForm[tt]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Now consider a more general conic:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ll = 
      PE[pp\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket], 
        pp\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]]; \n
    mm = PE[pp\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket], 
        pp\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket]]; \n
    gg = Tidy[a\ ff + b\ ll\ mm]\)], "Input"],

Cell["It is not generally degenerate:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[gg]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["It passes through the same four points:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\((PD[gg, #1\^2]&)\)/@pp]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Indeed, since we have a ",
  StyleBox["pencil of conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", this is the most general conic through the four points.  Here are the \
tangents:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ss = Tidy/@\(\((PD[gg, #1]&)\)/@pp\); \)\), 
    \(TableForm[ss]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have ",
  StyleBox["eight lines ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "on ",
  StyleBox["four points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We calculate a conic (in dual form) which has ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of these lines as tangents:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(hh = Tidy[Last[Factor[P2L@@Join[tt, Rest[ss]]]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This dual conic touches ",
  StyleBox["all eight lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\((PD[#1\^2, hh]&)\)/@Join[tt, ss]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is as good as a proof, since we can always choose the first conic and \
the four points as we please.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
And here is the conic with these eight tangents in point form.\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(kk = Factor/@Tidy[Dual[Envel[Dual[hh]]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["And here is one picture:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 1/3; b = 1; \n
    ImplicitPlot[{ff == 0, gg == 0, kk == 0, \n\t\t\ \ 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, \n\t\t\ \ 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, \n\t\t\ \ 
          ss\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, 
          ss\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, \n\t\t\ \ 
          ss\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, 
          ss\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0} /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, \n\t{x, \(-3\), 4}, {y, \(-3\), 8}, \n\t
      Axes \[Rule] None, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.005], \ Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], \ Green}, {
            Thickness[0.005], \ Red}, Black, Black, Black, Black, Black, 
          Black, Black, Black}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see additionaly that the conic ",
  StyleBox["kk",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " generally does not pass through the four points unless ",
  StyleBox["ff",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["gg",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b]; \nExpand[\((PD[kk, #1\^2]&)\)/@pp]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Correlations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Polarity with respect to a conic is a special case of a ",
  StyleBox["correlation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between points and lines.  Here is the general definition:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "A pair of non-singular linear mappings \n\t\t",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(CorP : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[u, v, w] \[Rule] 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "and\n\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(CorL : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[x, y, z] \[Rule] 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[u, v, w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nis said to be a ",
  StyleBox["correlation ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "provided that for all lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we have:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l, p] == PD[CorP[p], CorL[l]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is easy to argue by linear-algebra theory that either of the two \
mappings is ",
  StyleBox["uniquely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["determined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by the other, because the spaces are in a dual pairing and the inverse of \
one mapping is just the ",
  StyleBox["adjoint",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the other.  Indeed, correlations are a type of dual to projective \
transformations, in a sense we can make precise."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Recall that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Dual",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " was defined to ",
  StyleBox["interchange",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials with the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials by a subtitution of variables.  We can say more:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The pair of non-singular linear mappings \n\t\t",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Dual : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[u, v, w] \[Rule] 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[x, y, z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "and\n\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Dual : \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[x, y, z] \[Rule] 
          \(\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]\^1\)[u, v, w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a correlation",
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We should not be confused that the ",
  StyleBox["same name",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is being used for the two mappings, as the spaces are disjoint sets (save \
for the ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " element, which is mapped to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in any case)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point here is that ",
  StyleBox["polydifferentiation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["formally defined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " operation on polynomials that does not depend on which ",
  StyleBox["names",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are chosen for the variables.  We only have to pair ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". From then on, the computation of a differentiation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "depends only on the ",
  StyleBox["structure",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the polynomials (and their coefficients, of course).  Hence, the \
equation \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(Dual[PD[f, g]]\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(PD[Dual[g], Dual[f]]\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " \nholds for polynomials (of the appropriate types) of any degrees.  In \
the case of linear polynomials (where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[l, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a scalar), this shows that we have a correlation.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can also conclude that for any correlation, the composition ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Dual[CorP[p]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "is a projective transformation with the action on lines being given by the \
composition ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Dual[CorL[l]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since projective transformations can be computed by ",
  StyleBox["matrices",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and since generally ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Dual[Dual[f]] == f\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we find: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  For any pair of mappings ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorP",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorL",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " forming a correlation, there exists a unique non-singular matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " such that for all points and lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we can write: \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CorP[p] == Dual[\(TP[M]\)[p]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  and\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CorL[l] == Dual[\(TL[M]\)[l]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
There are two ways we can write the connection between matrices and \
correlations, and the following theorem gives us the exact connection between \
to two matrices needed.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  For any non-singular matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and for all points and lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Dual[\(TP[M]\)[p]] == \(TL[Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\)[Dual[p]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  and\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Dual[\(TL[M]\)[l]] == \(TP[Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\)[Dual[l]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Consider a general matrix and general point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[M, a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    M = {{a\_1, a\_2, a\_3}, {b\_1, b\_2, b\_3}, {c\_1, c\_2, c\_3}}; \n
    p = \ r\ u + s\ v + t\ w; \)], "Input"],

Cell["We compute first on a basis element:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Dual[\(TP[M]\)[u]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TL[Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\)[Dual[u]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Hmmm.  That does not look hopeful.  But, we can try a \
simplification.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(% // Together\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Whew!  It works.  And it ",
  StyleBox["should",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " work for the other basis elements.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Finally, we can tie up the connection with polarities.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "A pair of mappings ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorP",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorL",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " forming a correlation is said to be ",
  StyleBox["involutive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provided that for all points and lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have:\n\t     ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CorL[CorP[p]] == p\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CorP[CorL[l]] == l\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A pair of mappings ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorP",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["CorL",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " forming an involutive correlation is in fact a polarity with respect to a \
non-degenerate conic."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CorL[CorP[p]] == p\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " translates into the following according to what we proved earlier:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"Dual", "[", 
            RowBox[{\(TL[M]\), "[", 
              StyleBox[\(Dual[\(TP[M]\)[p]]\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\n",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\t\t",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(TP[Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\), "[", 
            RowBox[{"Dual", "[", 
              StyleBox[\(Dual[\(TP[M]\)[p]]\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
            StyleBox["]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\n",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\t\t",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(TP[Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\), 
            StyleBox["[",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(\(TP[M]\)[p]\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\t\t   ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\n",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["\t  ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(TP[TP[M] . Inverse[Transpose[M]]]\), 
            StyleBox["[",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["p",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means that \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M] . Inverse[Transpose[M]] == IdentityMatrix[3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nBut then we conclude that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M] == Transpose[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which is the same as being a ",
  StyleBox["symmetric matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Since ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-singular symmetric matrix ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the Hessian matrix of some conic, we can see that every involutive \
correlation comes from a conic.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Which conic corresponds to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Dual",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?  Here it is:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, Subscript]; \nf = 1\/2\ \((x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2)\); 
    \ng = Envel[f]\/2; \np = a\ u + b\ v + c\ w; \nl = a\ x + b\ y + c\ z; \n
    PD[f, p]\), 
    \(PD[l, g]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projectivities on a Conic", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining crossratios", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The crossratio of four points on a conic", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As we have remarked before, because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a linear operation, it follows  ",
  StyleBox["at once",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (!)",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "that if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a (non-degenerate) conic",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and if we have  four points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_4\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on a ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["PD",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["[",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["f",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["q",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
        StyleBox["]",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["PD",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["[",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["f",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["q",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
        StyleBox["]",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["PD",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["[",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["f",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["q",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
        StyleBox["]",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["PD",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["[",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["f",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["q",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
        StyleBox["]",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " are four ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lines in a ",
  StyleBox["pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (concurrent at the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the line).  Moreover, owing to the ",
  StyleBox["linearity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the transformation, the crossratio of the points must be ",
  StyleBox["the same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as the crossratio of the lines.  (Why?) "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is ",
  StyleBox["another fact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (noticed in special cases before) about equality of crossratios."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Construction.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be ",
  StyleBox["distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on a ",
  StyleBox["non-degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A line through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "intersects ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at a unique second point; hence, we can join the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to this point as well.",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The construction just defined gives a ",
  StyleBox["one-one correspondence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] determined by the ",
  StyleBox["choice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] between lines in the pencil at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and lines in the pencil at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The correspondence is ",
  StyleBox["projective",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and, hence, ",
  StyleBox["preserves crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof for a particular conic:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Consider the following conic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = x\ y + x\ z + y\ z; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["It passes through the vertices of the \"Basis Triangle\":", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{u, v, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The tangents at those points form another triangle in which the \
conic is inscribed:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{u, v, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here are the vertices of the circumscribed triangle:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PES[{y + z, x + z, x + y}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the lines joining these vertices to the original triple of \
points are ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[PE[w, u + v - w], PE[PE[u, \(-u\) + v + w], PE[v, u - v + w]]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now let us consider corresponding lines at the two points ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["v:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PES[{y + z, z, y}], PES[{z, x + z, x}]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Thread[F[{y + z, z, y}, {z, x + z, x}]] /. F \[Rule] PE\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now associating three lines in one pencil to three lines in the second \
pencil uniquely determines a ",
  StyleBox["projective correspondence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So, consider an arbitrary point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "in the plane.  We join the point to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and to ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and calculate the crossratios:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(aa = RL[y + z, z, y, PE[u, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(bb = RL[z, x + z, x, PE[v, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can check that these crossratios are equal if, and only if, the \
point lies on the conic by calculating the difference:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Together[bb - aa]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In terms of a crossratio ",
  StyleBox["a/b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we can also parameterize the points on the conic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(p = PE[XL[y + z, z, y, a, b], XL[z, x + z, x, a, b]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[f, p\^2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof for a symbolic conic:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We attempt a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic proof",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by declaring ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be a conic, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be two points on the conic, and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be the intersection of the tangents at those two points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p, q, r, s, a, b]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 2; \)\), 
    \(\(\((\((#1 /: DegP[#1] = 1)\)&)\)/@{p, q, r}; \)\), 
    \(\(p /: PD[PD[f, p], p] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(q /: PD[PD[f, q], q] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(r /: PD[PD[f, p], r] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(r /: PD[PD[f, q], r] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(q /: PD[PD[f, p], q] = c; \)\), 
    \(\(r /: PD[PD[f, r], r] = d; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be an arbitrary point on the tangent through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[":",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(s = a\ q + b\ r; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We then find the other point on the conic on the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t = Collect[PD[f, s\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[f, p\ s]\ s, {p, q, r}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We now compare the lines joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PE[p, t]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PE[q, t]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see at once that there is a ",
  StyleBox["linear transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " taking one line to the other.  This shows there is a projective \
correspondence betwee the pencil at ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and the pencil at ",
  StyleBox["q.  Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a conic, and if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_4\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are four points ",
  StyleBox["on the conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the ",
  StyleBox["crossratio",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the four points, written ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"RP2", "[", 
          RowBox[{"f", ",", 
            StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[" ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[" ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", 
            StyleBox[\(p\_4\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", can be calculated as a crossratio of four lines by using the tangent at \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as follows:\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"\t\t", 
        StyleBox[
          \(RP2[f_, p1_, p2_, p3_, p4_]\  := \ \n
            \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 
            RL[PD[f, p1], PE[p1, p2], PE[p1, p3], PE[p1, p4]]; \),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["We rerun the earlier example:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f]; \nf = x\ y + x\ z + y\ z; \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RP2[f, u, v, w, a\ \((\(-a\) + b)\)\ u + \((a - b)\)\ b\ v - a\ b\ w]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The following corollary follows directly from the above \
considerations.  (Why?)\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be five points, no three collinear.  Then the equation of the ",
  StyleBox["unique conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through these points can be written as\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["(",
            FontSize->14,
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          
          StyleBox[
            \(RL[PE[p, r], PE[p, s], PE[p, t], PE[p, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]] == 
              \ \ \ \ \ \n\t
              RL[PE[q, r], PE[q, s], PE[q, t], PE[q, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]]\),
            FontSize->14,
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[")",
            FontSize->14,
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["/.",
          FontSize->14,
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\({a \[Rule] x, b \[Rule] y, c \[Rule] z}\),
          FontSize->14,
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Pascal's Theorem", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["Here is the famous generalization of Pappus' Theorem:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If the six vertices ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_1, p\_2, p\_3, p\_4, p\_5, p\_6}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of a ",
  StyleBox["hexagon",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are placed on a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the three intersections of ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " pairs of sides are ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The six vertices of a hexagon need to be given ",
  StyleBox["in order",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  A different order is a different hexagon."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["First Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " By transformations, we can choose four points of the six points as the \
points of the quadrangle of reference."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[p, a, b, c, f, Subscript]; 
    \n{p\_1, p\_2, p\_3, p\_4, p\_5, p\_6} = {u, v, w, u + v + w, 
        a\_1\ u + a\_2\ v + a\_3\ w, b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w}; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The conic through ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the points is:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = Tidy[P2P[p\_1, p\_2, p\_3, p\_4, p\_5]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we compute the three intersections of the opposite sides:\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(q = Tidy[PE[PE[p\_1, p\_2], PE[p\_4, p\_5]]]\), 
    \(r = Tidy[PE[PE[p\_2, p\_3], PE[p\_5, p\_6]]]\), 
    \(s = Tidy[PE[PE[p\_3, p\_4], PE[p\_6, p\_1]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can then ask for the condition (which is a quantity that should be ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") that the three points be collinear:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[PE[q, r], s]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But let us also ask for the condition (= ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") that the sixth point is on the conic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\(-\(1\/2\)\)\ PD[f, p\_6\%2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_6\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the conic ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the ",
  StyleBox["3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are collinear!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.  (And a little more.)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Second Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " We procede ",
  StyleBox["without",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (This proof is taken from a textbook.)  We recall that a conic is the ",
  
  StyleBox[
  "locus of intersections of pairs of projectively related lines in two \
pencils",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (The conic is non-degenerate providing that the correspondence is not a \
perspectivity.)  The conic passes through the ",
  StyleBox["centers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two pencils.  Moreover ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conic can be obtained this way: just pick two points on the conic as \
centers, and relate the two pencils by joining the centers with points on the \
conic.  (This is the key result that justifies speaking of the crossratio of \
four points on a conic.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Well, suppose we have six points on a conic, which we will call\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_1, p\_2, p\_3, q\_1, q\_2, q\_3}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \nthis time.  Define the intersections of the opposite sides as follows:\n\
\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(r\_ij\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"PE", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "i"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "j"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "j"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "i"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ",\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["ij",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is replaced by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["12",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["23",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["31",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We have to show that these three points are collinear.  So let us define\
\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(axis == PE[r\_12, r\_31]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nThis line must cut the conic in at least one point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (which may be complex!).  Define in addition:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(r\_11\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"PE", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["axis",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" \t\t\n\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(r\_33\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"PE", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["axis",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as the center, the four points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the conic are projected onto the the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to the four points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_11\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_12\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_31\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then taking ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as the center, these same points on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are projected to the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  on the conic again.  And remember that these projections ",
  StyleBox["preserve crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can just as well take as centers ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   From ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we project the quadruple of points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the conic to the  points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_31\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_p\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_33\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where, of course, we define :\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"rp", "==", 
          RowBox[{"PE", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["axis",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  \nNext, from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we project the points   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_31\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_33\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where, similarly, we define: \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"rq", " ", "=", " ", 
          RowBox[{"PE", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["axis",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["PE",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"]}], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], "]"}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Because we already know that the crossratios are the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it follows that the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_p\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are the same.  But these points lie on the lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_3, q\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_2, q\_3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", respectively.",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Therefore, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_p == r\_q == r\_23\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and that means that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_23\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and, so, is collinear with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_12\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_31\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  Q.E.D.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "(",
  StyleBox["Whew! This proof needs a picture!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ")"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projective transformations", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projectivities on a conic", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
Having defined crossratio of four points on a conic, we can use \
that notion to define what we mean by projective transformation.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A ",
  StyleBox["projective transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or ",
  StyleBox["projectivity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") between points from one (non-degenerate) conic onto another is a one-one \
transformation that preserves crossratios."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Suppose two conics ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " intersect at a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  For any point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " find the other intersection of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This is a one-one correspondence between points on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and points on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (We just have to be a little careful to say what we mean when ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p == q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "is a tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)  Since crossratios are preserved from points on the first conic to \
lines in the pencil at  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and since crossratios are again preserved from the pencil to the second \
conic, this is indeed a projective transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is an example of ",
  StyleBox["tangent conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, t, Subscript]\), 
    \(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \ng = 4 \((x - 3  z)\)\^2 + 2  y\^2 - 16  z\^2; 
    \np = u + w; \n
    r[t_]\ \  := \((t\^2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\ v + \((t\^2 + 1)\)\ w; \n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, g == 0}, Table[PE[p, r[1/t]] == 0, {t, 1, 4}]] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-5\), 6}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the (non-singular) matrix for a projective transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of the plane onto itself, this transformation will be a projective \
transformation  in the sense of our definition of the points of one conic  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " onto the points of the image conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(TL[M]\)[f]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  (",
  StyleBox["Hint:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Set up a pencil at one point on the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and see what happens to that pencil under the transformation determined \
by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us prove the ",
  StyleBox["converse",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the observation of Example 2.  Note that the situation with ",
  StyleBox["conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is quite different from that with ",
  StyleBox["lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A projectivity from one non-degenerate conic onto another results from a \
",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projective transformation of the plane onto itself."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We argue in the way we did to prove that any two (non-degenerate) conics \
are projectively equivalent.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "be a triple of ",
  StyleBox["distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the first conic.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["p", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["q", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["r", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "be the triple of their ",
  StyleBox["images",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the second conic.  The points in the second triple are of course \
pairwise distinct.  Consider the tangents to the first conic at the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Those tangents are distinct and intersect at a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["not on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\tthe conic.  Consider the tangents to the second conic at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["p", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["q", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["s", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "be their intersection.  The two quadruples of points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, q, r, s}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["p", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["q", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["r", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["s", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are such that in each no three are collinear; hence, there is a ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projective transformation taking the first quadrangle onto the other."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This configuration of three points on a conic and the tangents at two of \
them, however, uniquely determines the conic \[LongDash] as we proved \
earlier.  Therefore, the projective transformation of the plane takes the \
first conic to the other.  But, on a conic (as on a line) three points and \
their images uniquely determin a projectivity.  S, the projective \
transformation of the plane has to agree with the given projectivity from one \
conic to the other.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As we know, projective transformations between lines can be given \
geometric constructions using (compositions) of perspectivities.  In the case \
of the transfomations of a conic onto itself, the construction is in a way ",
  StyleBox["much simpler",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We remark that a transformation with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["3",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed points, must be the identity transformation.  (Why?)  Thus, we can \
always suppose there are many pairs of ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " corresponding points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  If, on a fixed non-degenerate conic, the distinct pairs of distinct \
points  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond under a projective transformation of the conic onto itself, \
then the lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_1, q\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_2, q\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " intersect on a ",
  StyleBox["fixed line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (called the ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the transformation)."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Let  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a distinct ",
  StyleBox["third",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " corresponding pair.  Then we then have the data for ",
  StyleBox["Pascal's Theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and there is a common axis line determined by the intersections of the \
lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_i, q\_j]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p\_j, q\_i]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  As in the proof of the theorem, we can take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as centers of pencils and project the conic onto the axis and then back \
onto the conic again, preserving crossratios.  This makes ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " respectively.  But these two triples determine the transformation ",
  StyleBox["uniquely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". (Why?)  As the third pair can be chosen ",
  StyleBox["arbitrarily",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we can then conclude that the desired intersections always fall on the \
same axis.",
  StyleBox[" Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  By using the method of this theorem, any ",
  StyleBox["non-identity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projectivity of a conic onto itself can thus be uniquely determined ",
  StyleBox["and constructed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by giving the ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["one pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of distinct corresponding points (not on the axis).  Note that by this \
construction the",
  StyleBox[" intersections of the axis with the conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are exactly the ",
  StyleBox["fixed points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Note, too, that the axis and the pair of corresponding points may be \
chosen arbitrarily."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["Here is a picture for constructing one such projectivity.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, pp, qq, point, axis, Subscript]; \n
    f = x\^2 + y\^2 - 4  z\^2; \np = 2\ v + w; \nq = \(-2\) u + w; \n
    point = 3 \((v + w)\) + 2 \((u + w)\); \naxis = PE[v + w, u + w]; \n
    pp = PD[f, point\^2]\ q - 2\ PD[f, q\ point]\ point; \n
    qq = PD[f, point\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[f, p\ point]\ point; \n
    ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, axis == 0, \n\t\tPE[p, point] == 0, 
          PE[q, point] == 0} /. {z -> 1}, \n\t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, 
      \nPlotStyle -> {{Thickness[0.005], \ Blue}, {Thickness[0.005], Red}, 
          Black, Black}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Any non-identity projectivity of a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " onto itself has only ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " fixed points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Any non-identity projectivity of a ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " onto itself has only ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " fixed points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "From this construction we can also prove a kind of ",
  StyleBox["converse",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to Pascal's Theorem."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  If six points \[LongDash] no three collinear \[LongDash] forming a \
hexagon have the intersections of opposite pairs of sides collinear, then the \
six points lie on a conic."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Take the six points in order around the hexagon as in this list ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["q",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], ",", \(q\_1\), ",", \(p\_2\), ",", 
            \(q\_3\)}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ". The five points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["{",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], ",", 
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["q",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], ",", 
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], ",", \(q\_1\), ",", \(p\_2\)}],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["}",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "determine a non-degenerate conic.  Use the line joining the intersections \
of the opposite pairs of sides as an ",
  StyleBox["axis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for a projectivity on the conic making ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond.  Then the construction ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the axis will make ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond.  But, this puts  on the conic!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We had already proved this conclusion by symbolic computation in the \
first proof of Pascal's Theorem.  (Why?)"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
We can also use the construction of projectivities to prove another \
theorem about dual conics.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The joins of corresponding points under a projectivity on a non- \
degenerate conic envelop another conic."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " We divide the calculation into ",
  StyleBox["two cases",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 1:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The axis is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tangent to the conic.  In this case we can transform the configuration so \
that the axis is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == PE[u, v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the conic, and so that one pair of corresponding points is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(w \[Rule] u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The general form of the conic passing through these four points is \
easily seen to be these conics forming a pencil: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ y \((x - z)\) + b\ z \((x - y)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\(a\ y \((x - z)\) + b\ z \((x - y)\)\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            SuperscriptBox[
              StyleBox["#",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], 
          StyleBox["]",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["&",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "/@", 
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w, u + v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, we carry out the construction of the projectivity using variable \
points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s\ u + t\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{\(ClearAll[f, p, q, pp, qq, a, b, s, t, Subscript]\), ";", 
      RowBox[{"f", "=", 
        StyleBox[\(a\ y \((x - z)\) + b\ z \((x - y)\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
      StyleBox[";",
        FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", \(p = w\), ";", "\n", \(q = u + v + w\), 
      ";", "\n", 
      \(pp = PD[f, \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\^2]\ q - 
          2\ PD[f, q\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)]\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\), ";", "\n", 
      \(qq = PD[f, \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\^2]\ p - 
          2\ PD[f, p\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)]\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\), ";", "\n", 
      "\n", \(line = Map[Factor, Tidy/@Factor[PE[pp, qq]], {3}]\)}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The formula for the moving line is not so bad.  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we have a degenerate conic, so we can drop that factor.  If either ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the moving point on the axis is in a special position, and it would seem \
the the rest of the formula takes care of that case.  So, we drop those \
factors as well:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(line = Last[line]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We then compute five of the lines:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(five = \(\((line /. {t -> 1, s -> #})\)&\)/@Range[5]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we can find the dual conic which is touched by these five \
lines.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dual = Factor/@Last[Tidy/@Factor[P2L@@five]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The formula is simple enough!  Let us check that the general form \
of the moving line touches that conic:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[line\^2, dual] // Expand\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It does!  That proves the theorem in this case.  We can also find the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-form of the conic to which all these lines are tangent:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(touch = Dual[Envel[Dual[dual]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["When is this conic degenerate?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MHP[touch]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We already know ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is bad.  What about ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == \(-2\) b\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(touch /. {a \[Rule] 2, b \[Rule] \(-1\)} // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dual /. {a \[Rule] 2, b \[Rule] \(-1\)} // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ah, all the lines have to pass through the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + 2\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This means that the projectivity is very special \[LongDash] and we \
look at that special case in the next section."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 2:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The axis ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tangent to the conic.  In this case we can transform the configuration so \
that the axis is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the point of tangency, and so that one pair of corresponding points is, \
say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + w \[Rule] v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The general form of the conic satisfying these conditions is easily \
seen to be these conics forming a pencil: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c\ y \((y - z)\) + d\ z \((x + y - z)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{\(ClearAll[c, d]\), ";", "\n", 
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            StyleBox[\(c\ y \((y - z)\) + d\ z \((x + y - z)\), #\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
          StyleBox["&",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["/@",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["{",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox[
              StyleBox["u",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], ",", "u", ",", \(\((u + w)\)\^2\), 
            ",", \(\((v + w)\)\^2\)}], "}"}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["We do the similar calculations as before.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{\(ClearAll[f, p, q, pp, qq, c, d, s, t, Subscript]\), ";", 
      RowBox[{"f", "=", 
        StyleBox[\(c\ y \((y - z)\) + d\ z \((x + y - z)\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
      StyleBox[";",
        FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", \(p = u + w\), ";", "\n", \(q = v + w\), 
      ";", "\n", 
      \(pp = PD[f, \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\^2]\ q - 
          2\ PD[f, q\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)]\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\), ";", "\n", 
      \(qq = PD[f, \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\^2]\ p - 
          2\ PD[f, p\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)]\ \((s\ u + t\ v)\)\), ";", "\n", 
      "\n", \(line = Map[Factor, Tidy/@Factor[PE[pp, qq]], {3}]\)}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(line = Last[line]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(five = \(\((line /. {t -> 1, s -> #})\)&\)/@Range[5]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dual = Factor/@Last[Tidy/@Factor[P2L@@five]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[line\^2, dual] // Expand\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(touch = Dual[Envel[Dual[dual]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MHP[touch]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(d == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are bad, but otherwise the desired result holds.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["We attempt a picture to illustrate the theorem.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = \(-8\); d = 2; \n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, touch == 0}, 
          \((line == 0 /. {t \[Rule] 1, s \[Rule] #1}&)\)/@
            \((2  Range[\(-3\), 3])\)] /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 10}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tPlotPoints -> 100, \ \n\t
      Axes \[Rule] None, \n
      PlotStyle -> {{Thickness[0.004], Red}, \n\t{Thickness[0.004], Blue}, \n
          \t\t\tBlack, Black, Black, Black, \n\t\t\tBlack, Black, Black}]; 
    \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The conic touched by the lines joining corresponding points is also \
tangent to the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[touch, u]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Involutions on a conic", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  An ",
  StyleBox["involution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a projective transformation of a projective space onto itself which ",
  StyleBox["is not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the identity but whose composition with itself ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the identity transformation.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is mapped to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " under an involution, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must be mapped to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We say that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " have been ",
  StyleBox["interchanged",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by the transformation."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A projective transformation of a conic onto itself which interchanges  ",
  
  StyleBox["one pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of distinct points is in fact an ",
  StyleBox["involution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be distinct points that are interchanged by a given  transformation.  Let \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a third point (that is also not one of the two possible fixed points), \
and let it be mapped to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We have four distinct points on the conic forming a quadrangle.  By the \
theorem on harmonic properties, the ",
  StyleBox["diagonal points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the quadrangle form a ",
  StyleBox["self-polar triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with respect to the given conic.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Define:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(center == PE[PE[p\_1, q\_1], PE[p\_2, q\_2]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["\n\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["axis",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["PE",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["[",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["PE",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["[",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["PE",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["[",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["p",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                    StyleBox[",",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["q",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], 
                  StyleBox["]",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox[",",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["PE",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["[",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["p",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
                    StyleBox[",",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["q",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"]}], 
                  StyleBox["]",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["\n",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["\t\t              ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["PE",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["[",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["PE",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["[",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["p",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                    StyleBox[",",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["p",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], 
                  StyleBox["]",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox[",",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["PE",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox["[",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["q",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                    StyleBox[",",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["q",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], 
                  StyleBox["]",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
          StyleBox["]",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Then using projections from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " onto the axis and back, we see that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are interchanged.  But, because the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["center",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", this construction of the transformation will interchange ",
  StyleBox["any other pair",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of corresponding points.",
  StyleBox["  Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
We have actually proved more about involutions in the above \
argument. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The lines joining ",
  StyleBox["corresponding points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of an involution on a conic all pass through the ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (which gives an easy ",
  StyleBox["construction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of any involution).  Hence, an involution on a conic has ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " two fixed points (which may be complex).  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let two given non-singular conics pass through a common point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a point ",
  StyleBox["not on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " either of them.  Consider the the ",
  StyleBox["pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of lines through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Any line in this pencil cuts the first conic in two points.  The two \
lines through these two points from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cut the second conic in two further points.  We can prove that the lines \
joining these two points form a ",
  StyleBox["second pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through a common point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  First we draw a picture."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, t, Subscript]\), 
    \(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \ng = \((x - 3  z)\)\^2 + y\^2 - 4  z\^2; \n
    p = u + w; \nq = 2  u + 3  v + w; \n
    r[t_]\ \  := \((t\^2 - 1)\)\ u + 2\ t\ v + \((t\^2 + 1)\)\ w; \n
    s[t_]\ \  := PD[f, q\^2]\ r[t] - 2  PD[f, r[t]\ q]\ q; \n
    rr[t_] := PD[g, r[t]\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[g, p\ r[t]]\ r[t]; \n
    ss[t_] := PD[g, s[t]\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[g, p\ s[t]]\ s[t]; \)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0, g == 0}, 
          Flatten[Table[{PE[q, r[t]] == 0, PE[p, r[t]] == 0, 
                PE[p, s[t]] == 0, PE[rr[t], ss[t]] == 0}, {t, 1, 3}]]] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-5\), 6}, {y, \(-7\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Of course, the pairs of points on the small circle are pairs in an \
involution, and the transfer through the tangent point makes an involution on \
the large circle.  Hence, by the general theorem, the joins of corresponding \
pairs have to form a pencil.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["We can also argue for this result by symbolic computation.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f, g, p, q, r, s, t, Subscript]\), 
    \(f /: DegP[f] = 2; \ng /: DegP[g] = 2; \np /: DegP[p] = 1; \n
    q /: DegP[q] = 1; \nr /: DegP[r] = 1; \np /: PD[PD[f, p], p] = 0; \n
    p /: PD[PD[g, p], p] = 0; \nr /: PD[PD[f, r], r] = 0; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(rr = \(-PD[f, q\^2]\) r + 2  PD[f, q\ r] q; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, rr\^2] // ExpandAll\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(s = \ \ \ \ \ \(-PD[g, r\^2]\) p + 2  PD[g, r\ p] r\n\), 
    \(ss = Collect[\(-PD[g, rr\^2]\) p + 2  PD[g, rr\ p] rr, {p, q, r}]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Collect[PE[s, ss], {PE[p, q], PE[p, r], PE[q, r]}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Any projectivity on a conic (and, therefore, on a line) is the product of \
",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " involutions (in infinitely many ways)."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Let the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the axis for the projectivity on the conic, and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be points on the conic ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the axis (non-fixed points), where the first is mapped to the second \
under the projectivity.    "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " any point on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " not on the conic.  Join ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and find where the line intersects the conic.  From that point draw a \
line through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and find where the line intersects ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Call this point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as centers for two involutions on the conic, we see that, by \
construction, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is mapped to",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " under the product of the two involutions.  Moreover, the two fixed points \
are left fixed under the product of the two involutions.  Therefore, if the \
fixed points are  ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the product of the two involutions is the desired projectivity. (Why? \
Because the images of three distinct points determine a one-dimensonal \
projective mapping.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "On the other hand, if the fixed points are ",
  StyleBox["equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (and so the axis is a ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the conic), then take another pair ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in the projectivity.  We know the lines joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " meet on the axis.  A simple application of Pascal's Theorem shows that \
the product of the involutions mapps ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " So the product of the involutions ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the projectivity. (A picture would be useful!)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This construction started with an arbitrary choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["axis",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, there are infinitely many ways in which the decomposition can be \
done.",
  StyleBox[" Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
